-- title:
--   Finitely generated plus torsion gives the Eisenstein finiteness at p
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, and equip the degree-zero Picard group $J =$ `JZero p` $= \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar p` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with the `heckeModuleBar p` module structure over $\mathbb{T} =$ `HeckeAlg` $= \mathrm{MvPolynomial}\ \mathrm{Nat.Primes}\ \mathbb{Z}$: when all the operators `heckeOperatorBar p ℓ` ($\ell$ prime) commute with one another, the variable indexed by $\ell$ acts as `heckeOperatorBar p ℓ`, and otherwise all variables act as $0$. Write $K =$ `eisensteinKernel` $J$ `(eisensteinIdeal p)` $\cdot \top$ for the `HeckeAlg`-submodule of $J$ generated by that subset, and let $R \subseteq J/K$ be the image under the quotient map of $\{x \in J : \sigma \cdot x - x \in K \text{ for all } \sigma \in \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})\}$, the set `eisensteinQuotientRational p (heckeModuleBar p)`. Assume that the Hecke operators `heckeOperatorBar p` commute pairwise, that the additive subgroup of $J/K$ generated by $R$ is finitely generated, and that every $z \in R$ satisfies $n \cdot z = 0$ for some integer $n > 0$. The conclusion is `EisensteinQuotientInvariantsFiniteAt p (heckeModuleBar p)`, namely that the set $R$ is finite.
--
--   This is the finiteness assertion for the Galois-invariant part of the Eisenstein quotient of $J_0(p)$ that enters Mazur's argument, here reduced to two separate inputs about the set of rational points of that quotient. It is cited by [`ModularCurve.eisensteinQuotientInvariantsFiniteAt_heckeModuleBar`](thm.html#ModularCurve.eisensteinQuotientInvariantsFiniteAt_heckeModuleBar), where the two hypotheses are supplied in turn.
